1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,960
They're designed for a single purpose.

2
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:07,060
To kill with maximum efficiency.

3
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:11,560
From flying shrapnel to rapid-fire flying bullets.

4
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:14,820
From irradiation to incineration

5
00:00:14,821 --> 00:00:18,560
to a microscopic drop of a lethal chemical agent.

6
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:21,720
These weapons all have one thing in common.

7
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:25,240
If you're in their crosshairs, there's nowhere to hide.

8
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:29,380
Now, Deadliest Weapons on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:45,169 --> 00:00:52,540
On a cloudy fall day, a Soviet Tu-95 bomber flies over the island Novaya Zimla, located in the Arctic Sea.

10
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:57,360
Reaching a height of 35,000 feet, the pilot drops his ordnance.

11
00:00:57,361 --> 00:01:03,300
the Tsar Bomba, a 50 megaton nuclear bomb.

12
00:01:04,380 --> 00:01:06,780
Within moments, the fusion device detonates,

13
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:09,360
shooting a flash of light through the atmosphere,

14
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:12,080
visible from 600 miles away.

15
00:01:17,020 --> 00:01:21,960
It was and remains the largest nuclear detonation in history.

16
00:01:21,961 --> 00:01:24,720
It was originally designed to be 100 megatons.

17
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:31,140
It very nearly killed the senior officer who was piloting the aircraft that dropped the test article.

18
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:38,240
Capable of destroying everything within a 15-mile radius

19
00:01:38,241 --> 00:01:42,540
and delivering third-degree burns within a 64-mile radius,

20
00:01:44,020 --> 00:01:48,180
the Tsar Bomba remains the final word in total annihilation of an enemy target.

21
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:53,620
Today, the Tsar Bomba is but a reminder of the Cold War arms race

22
00:01:53,621 --> 00:01:55,960
and the danger of an all-out nuclear war

23
00:01:55,961 --> 00:01:58,120
that would have likely caused the destruction

24
00:01:58,121 --> 00:02:00,220
of both the U.S. and Soviet Union,

25
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:01,980
if not the world.

26
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:08,500
Yet the threat of nuclear attack

27
00:02:08,501 --> 00:02:10,240
from our former adversary remains.

28
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:13,120
The risk of accidental attack

29
00:02:13,121 --> 00:02:15,340
is more serious, I think,

30
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:17,640
than most people realize.

31
00:02:18,860 --> 00:02:21,240
The Russians maintain

32
00:02:21,241 --> 00:02:23,560
a large nuclear arsenal

33
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:29,040
Since the collapse of the Soviet Union, they've had a huge economic setback.

34
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:33,180
Their warning system has deteriorated terribly.

35
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:35,440
There are gaps in their radars.

36
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:37,780
Their satellites have broken down.

37
00:02:38,940 --> 00:02:42,420
The Russians, of course, reassure us that, no, we have nothing to worry about.

38
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:49,000
But the consequences would be so grave that we should take it very, very seriously,

39
00:02:49,001 --> 00:02:51,740
even if it has a minuscule chance of happening at all.

40
00:02:53,560 --> 00:03:00,360
The threat of a nuclear attack from a rogue nation or a well-organized stateless group is also cause for grave concern.

41
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:05,100
Whether North Korea has nuclear weapons is very much in the press today.

42
00:03:05,660 --> 00:03:09,600
Should terrorists get their hands on sufficient nuclear materials,

43
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:12,300
either to make a non-nuclear weapon, a radiological weapon,

44
00:03:12,840 --> 00:03:19,060
or to actually get a nuclear detonation, however small, would be a horrendous attack.

45
00:03:19,061 --> 00:03:26,900
The evolution of precision guidance systems has eliminated the need for large-yield nuclear

46
00:03:26,901 --> 00:03:29,240
weapons like the Tsar Bomba.

47
00:03:29,241 --> 00:03:37,260
But make no mistake, the detonation of a one-megaton nuclear bomb would yield more destructive

48
00:03:37,261 --> 00:03:43,820
power than any weapon the world has ever seen.

49
00:03:43,821 --> 00:03:46,820
weapon, thermonuclear warhead.

50
00:03:46,821 --> 00:03:50,820
Yield one megaton surface blast.

51
00:03:50,821 --> 00:03:52,820
Energy released?

52
00:03:52,821 --> 00:03:55,820
Blast, 50%.

53
00:03:55,821 --> 00:03:58,820
Thermal radiation, 40%.

54
00:03:58,821 --> 00:04:02,820
Fallout, 10%.

55
00:04:02,821 --> 00:04:06,820
To comprehend the true lethality of a thermonuclear explosion

56
00:04:06,821 --> 00:04:09,820
requires an understanding of the massive amount of energy

57
00:04:09,821 --> 00:04:12,820
that's released upon detonation.

58
00:04:13,820 --> 00:04:23,040
When a thermonuclear bomb detonates, an instantaneous fission reaction creates a secondary fusion reaction,

59
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:30,400
fusing hydrogen and its isotopes together to form helium, releasing enormous amounts of energy.

60
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:33,380
And it occurs in a very small volume.

61
00:04:33,920 --> 00:04:37,780
Therefore, the total energy divided by the volume is very large.

62
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:41,440
And that means the temperatures are stupendously high.

63
00:04:41,441 --> 00:04:44,240
temperatures that are higher than the center of the sun.

64
00:04:46,700 --> 00:04:51,060
Upon detonation, a thermonuclear bomb emits a stream of X-rays,

65
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:53,540
infrared rays, and gamma rays.

66
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:58,220
This is referred to as thermal radiation

67
00:04:58,221 --> 00:05:02,400
and is visible to the naked eye in the form of a brilliant flash of light

68
00:05:02,401 --> 00:05:04,940
lasting from 1 to 10 seconds.

69
00:05:05,780 --> 00:05:10,380
It emits all of this X-radiation that's absorbed by the air around it.

70
00:05:10,381 --> 00:05:14,700
The outside of the air is burning. It's forming basically smog.

71
00:05:14,840 --> 00:05:17,700
The nitrogen and the oxygen are reacting. It turns brown,

72
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:22,100
and so the light coming in actually goes down until that burns off, and then it goes up again.

73
00:05:23,540 --> 00:05:27,600
The heat from the infrared rays will set people and buildings on fire.

74
00:05:29,060 --> 00:05:32,220
The X-rays will irradiate those closest to the explosion.

75
00:05:32,700 --> 00:05:35,800
The X-rays don't get very far. They're absorbed by the air.

76
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:39,840
The air is then heated up by these X-rays to millions of degrees.

77
00:05:40,380 --> 00:05:47,380
The immense heat expands the air around the point of impact, creating a spherical shockwave

78
00:05:47,780 --> 00:05:50,380
and winds that can reach hundreds of miles per hour.

79
00:05:50,381 --> 00:05:54,980
And if it were a humid day, you'd be able to see that shockwave running along the ground

80
00:05:54,981 --> 00:06:00,280
and through the air because it would cause instant condensation of moisture in the atmosphere

81
00:06:00,281 --> 00:06:05,280
and you'd see sort of a white ghost of a shockwave traveling through the air.

82
00:06:05,281 --> 00:06:11,460
The wind from the shockwave extinguishes the fires caused by the thermal radiation,

83
00:06:11,960 --> 00:06:15,660
but will flatten everything in its path within a radius of two miles.

84
00:06:18,620 --> 00:06:24,640
You get the expansion out, but then the thermal plume of the bomb is rising.

85
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:25,940
The mushroom cloud is forming.

86
00:06:28,080 --> 00:06:33,040
And so you'll get a counterflow, which when air flows up, it sucks things in,

87
00:06:33,041 --> 00:06:38,540
And so you get a reversal of the flow as things flow back toward the detonation center and rise.

88
00:06:40,260 --> 00:06:45,140
A one megaton surface explosion will lift tons of soil up into the mushroom cloud,

89
00:06:45,420 --> 00:06:48,980
which will become irradiated and return as fallout.

90
00:06:50,840 --> 00:06:54,660
The heaviest fallout particles will rain down closest to ground zero.

91
00:06:55,940 --> 00:07:00,520
People exposed to this fallout will die in a matter of hours from acute radiation sickness.

92
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:07,400
The smaller particles may travel thousands of miles, depending on wind velocity.

93
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:11,380
If you ingest the material and it stays in your body,

94
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:17,460
the long-term impact is that it can produce significant excess cancer of various sorts.

95
00:07:18,680 --> 00:07:28,400
The combination of thermal radiation, shockwave, and fallout could potentially kill millions,

96
00:07:28,401 --> 00:07:33,400
making the thermonuclear bomb easily the world's deadliest weapon.

97
00:07:36,220 --> 00:07:40,640
By 2004, the nuclear fraternity consisted of seven countries

98
00:07:40,641 --> 00:07:43,200
that had all detonated nuclear devices.

99
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:47,780
Other countries were desperately working toward joining the family.

100
00:07:49,560 --> 00:07:52,620
Thus, there appears to be no end in sight to nuclear proliferation

101
00:07:52,621 --> 00:07:57,660
and no end to the threat of attack from the world's deadliest weapon.

102
00:07:58,400 --> 00:08:03,700
According to the Center for Defense Information,

103
00:08:04,360 --> 00:08:08,240
there are approximately 30,000 intact nuclear warheads worldwide.

104
00:08:09,320 --> 00:08:12,420
All but 200 are retained by the United States and Russia.

105
00:08:15,540 --> 00:08:18,960
Deadliest Weapons will return on Modern Marvels.

106
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:25,340
It's one of the deadliest chemical agents ever created.

107
00:08:25,341 --> 00:08:30,920
Within seconds of exposure, symptoms of nausea and convulsions take over.

108
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:38,020
Without emergency treatment, paralysis, respiratory failure and death can occur within minutes.

109
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:42,040
This deadly killer is the nerve agent VX.

110
00:08:43,420 --> 00:08:51,840
Weapon VX. Classification organophosphate. Lethal dose 30 micrograms.

111
00:08:51,841 --> 00:08:57,840
The organophosphate type of chemicals are considered nerve agents

112
00:08:57,841 --> 00:09:00,000
because they attack the nervous system.

113
00:09:00,080 --> 00:09:02,920
The nervous system controls all of the functions of the body,

114
00:09:03,640 --> 00:09:07,980
and so by interrupting or stimulating that, you get all these various effects.

115
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:13,940
VX in its normal state is a tasteless, odorless liquid

116
00:09:13,941 --> 00:09:16,300
that can be absorbed through the skin in seconds.

117
00:09:17,300 --> 00:09:22,200
When heated, it turns into a lingering vapor that, if inhaled, is even more deadly.

118
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:28,760
VX is a persistent agent, which means it's less volatile than the other nerve agents.

119
00:09:28,860 --> 00:09:36,940
This means that when it's exposed to the air, the material doesn't go up into the air, and it lasts much longer.

120
00:09:38,140 --> 00:09:44,360
To give you an idea of how toxic the chemical is, if you pulled a penny out of your pocket,

121
00:09:44,361 --> 00:09:47,960
and if you looked at the penny and you looked at Lincoln's eye,

122
00:09:48,520 --> 00:09:54,080
it only takes a drop the size of Lincoln's eye to cause lethality.

123
00:09:55,620 --> 00:09:59,420
There are no confirmed cases of VX being used on people,

124
00:10:00,100 --> 00:10:05,500
but it's widely believed that during Saddam Hussein's chemical attack against the Kurds in 1988,

125
00:10:06,880 --> 00:10:09,080
VX was dispersed with deadly results.

126
00:10:09,081 --> 00:10:14,880
the attack on the Kurds killed over 5,000

127
00:10:14,881 --> 00:10:20,340
VX is the most potent nerve agent today

128
00:10:20,341 --> 00:10:24,680
but the path to its discovery dates back to before World War I

129
00:10:24,681 --> 00:10:30,020
chemists knew that many of the chemicals used in the dye industry

130
00:10:30,021 --> 00:10:33,640
and other industries could be very deadly

131
00:10:33,641 --> 00:10:40,420
During World War I, the Germans began to weaponize chemicals

132
00:10:40,421 --> 00:10:43,860
by putting in artillery shells and portable chemical cylinders

133
00:10:43,861 --> 00:10:45,840
and releasing them on the battlefield.

134
00:10:46,680 --> 00:10:48,020
And they used them very effectively.

135
00:10:49,420 --> 00:10:52,240
Although chemical agents were effective in causing casualties,

136
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:55,860
they were not always lethal.

137
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,520
But that would change in the 1930s,

138
00:10:59,521 --> 00:11:03,720
when German scientists created the first nerve agent named Tabin.

139
00:11:05,220 --> 00:11:11,040
Between 1942 and 1945, they produced 12,000 tons of Tabin,

140
00:11:11,200 --> 00:11:13,900
as well as several thousand variations, including sarin.

141
00:11:14,120 --> 00:11:16,560
The nerve agents were definitely unique

142
00:11:16,561 --> 00:11:21,040
compared to the earlier World War I chemical warfare agents.

143
00:11:21,620 --> 00:11:23,560
They had very little smell to them.

144
00:11:24,240 --> 00:11:26,440
They began affecting the person very quickly

145
00:11:26,441 --> 00:11:29,040
and were much more lethal.

146
00:11:29,520 --> 00:11:37,520
When Germany fell to the Allies in 1945, its chemical stockpile was seized, and the United

147
00:11:37,521 --> 00:11:43,480
States began producing its own variation of the German nerve agents, which became classified

148
00:11:43,481 --> 00:11:46,360
as G-agents.

149
00:11:46,361 --> 00:11:51,680
The most widely produced was a far more lethal version of sarin nerve gas.

150
00:11:51,681 --> 00:11:55,400
But by the early 1950s, an even deadlier nerve agent was discovered.

151
00:11:55,401 --> 00:12:02,300
The British company was investigating some insecticides and came across a particularly potent one.

152
00:12:02,720 --> 00:12:09,060
They looked at it and then referred it to the United States in about 1953-54 time frame.

153
00:12:09,780 --> 00:12:18,080
And the United States looked at it and realized that there was a whole new series of very potent nerve agents that had been discovered.

154
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:26,200
They looked at all of them and then decided that probably VX was the one that they would like to go with.

155
00:12:29,580 --> 00:12:37,500
From 1961 to 1968, the United States produced approximately 4,400 tons of VX,

156
00:12:37,860 --> 00:12:40,040
enough to kill every human being on the planet.

157
00:12:40,860 --> 00:12:44,440
The U.S. stockpile served as a deterrent against the Soviet Union,

158
00:12:44,880 --> 00:12:47,980
which had begun producing even larger supplies of chemical agents.

159
00:12:48,080 --> 00:12:52,620
including the nerve agents Soman, Saren, and VX.

160
00:12:53,500 --> 00:12:59,640
In 1960 and 61, we standardized an artillery projectile

161
00:12:59,641 --> 00:13:04,460
and we came up with a newly designed landmine

162
00:13:04,461 --> 00:13:10,780
and also loaded VX in a rocket warhead.

163
00:13:12,920 --> 00:13:16,880
Fortunately, neither country ever used a lethal nerve agent against the other.

164
00:13:18,080 --> 00:13:24,400
In 1969, Richard Nixon agreed to ban the U.S. manufacturing of chemical weapons.

165
00:13:25,780 --> 00:13:29,360
Production of VX ceased, and the stockpile was placed in storage.

166
00:13:30,940 --> 00:13:35,100
Today, only the United States and Russia claim to have stockpiles of VX.

167
00:13:37,540 --> 00:13:42,120
Yet some suspect that Iraq may have produced large quantities of the deadly nerve agent.

168
00:13:42,121 --> 00:13:50,280
At the onset of Operation Iraqi Freedom, U.S. forces prepared for the worst.

169
00:13:50,600 --> 00:13:57,000
We felt that it was very likely that our adversaries would use chemical weapons against us,

170
00:13:57,100 --> 00:14:00,220
and we felt that they had VX in their arsenal.

171
00:14:01,380 --> 00:14:07,700
VX is often used as a terrain denial weapon, kind of the way you would use landmines.

172
00:14:07,701 --> 00:14:16,580
And one of the things we're very concerned about is not having our forces stumble into land that's been contaminated with VX.

173
00:14:17,280 --> 00:14:24,871
The main goal at Edgewood Chemical and Biological Center in Maryland is to provide protection and detection for the U.S.

174
00:14:24,872 --> 00:14:25,820
troops in Iraq.

175
00:14:26,260 --> 00:14:29,520
We have the best protective force in the world.

176
00:14:30,060 --> 00:14:37,360
And each warfighter has all the protective equipment that he or she needs, the protective clothing and the protective mask.

177
00:14:37,700 --> 00:14:41,700
The masks will totally protect you against inhaling VX,

178
00:14:41,980 --> 00:14:48,940
and the chemical protective clothing is specially designed so that the VX can't penetrate it.

179
00:14:49,440 --> 00:14:55,740
In January of 2005, the United States ended its hunt for weapons of mass destruction in Iraq.

180
00:14:57,040 --> 00:14:58,680
No VX was found.

181
00:14:59,600 --> 00:15:02,220
But the threat of VX doesn't end in Iraq.

182
00:15:03,100 --> 00:15:06,660
Other countries are suspected to be attempting to produce the deadly agent.

183
00:15:07,700 --> 00:15:12,240
And there is enough VX in the U.S. and Russian stockpiles to kill millions.

184
00:15:13,400 --> 00:15:17,620
So the threat of attack from one of the world's deadliest weapons remains.

185
00:15:20,880 --> 00:15:26,220
The most effective antidote to VX is a mixture of drugs that includes atropine,

186
00:15:26,980 --> 00:15:30,740
prolidoxine chloride, and diazepam.

187
00:15:31,900 --> 00:15:35,284
Auto-injector kits containing these drugs are given to U.S.

188
00:15:35,285 --> 00:15:37,120
soldiers in case of a VX attack.

189
00:15:37,700 --> 00:15:42,700
Deadliest Weapons will return on Modern Marvels.

190
00:15:48,700 --> 00:15:53,700
The machine gun. It's not just a weapon. It's a cultural icon.

191
00:15:53,701 --> 00:15:56,700
For some, it's even a nickname.

192
00:15:56,701 --> 00:16:02,700
But in its essence, it's a lethal killing machine.

193
00:16:02,701 --> 00:16:06,700
With the ability to fire hundreds of rounds of ammunition in seconds,

194
00:16:06,701 --> 00:16:10,960
The machine gun has remained a constant on the battlefield since the Civil War.

195
00:16:13,380 --> 00:16:15,560
But of all the wars in which it's been used,

196
00:16:16,200 --> 00:16:21,280
it's the Maxim machine gun of World War I that brought the weapon to the apex of its lethality.

197
00:16:23,540 --> 00:16:26,780
Weapon. Maxim heavy machine gun.

198
00:16:27,860 --> 00:16:30,660
Rate of fire, 500 rounds per minute.

199
00:16:31,620 --> 00:16:33,720
Range, 4,000 yards.

200
00:16:33,721 --> 00:16:43,320
The first functional rapid-fire weapon was the Gatling gun, invented by physician Richard Jordan Gatling in 1862.

201
00:16:46,780 --> 00:16:52,360
But the rapid-fire killing potential of Gatling's weapon was limited by its rather impractical design.

202
00:16:53,840 --> 00:16:55,828
The Gatling gun was heavy.

203
00:16:55,829 --> 00:17:01,640
It was prone to malfunction with the cranking, the cartridges getting stuck.

204
00:17:02,200 --> 00:17:07,280
It wasn't exactly the kind of handy thing that you move across the battlefield at a good clip.

205
00:17:08,140 --> 00:17:13,500
And in fact, all the early Gatling guns were horse-drawn, so that certainly limited their mobility.

206
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:17,760
The Gatling gun was largely abandoned by 1883,

207
00:17:18,540 --> 00:17:23,340
when another American, Hiram Maxim, invented the first completely automatic weapon.

208
00:17:24,420 --> 00:17:30,420
He was a man with a very fertile mind, and in 1881, he went to London.

209
00:17:30,421 --> 00:17:34,760
while there one of his friends reportedly said to him

210
00:17:34,761 --> 00:17:40,020
Hiram why don't you develop a new way for Europeans to kill each other

211
00:17:40,021 --> 00:17:42,620
they're always at each other's throats you can make a lot of money

212
00:17:42,621 --> 00:17:46,400
he kind of said oh okay I'll invent some guns

213
00:17:46,401 --> 00:17:49,920
and within a couple of years

214
00:17:49,921 --> 00:17:54,460
I mean it took that little time he had invented the machine gun

215
00:17:54,461 --> 00:18:04,340
The automatic firing system of the Maxim machine gun consisted of an action, a reaction, and

216
00:18:04,341 --> 00:18:05,340
an action.

217
00:18:05,341 --> 00:18:12,640
When a round fired, it produced a recoil force that loaded the next round in place while simultaneously

218
00:18:12,641 --> 00:18:17,900
ejecting the spent cartridge.

219
00:18:17,901 --> 00:18:22,620
Maxim licensed his design to the British company Vickers, which quickly produced the Vickers

220
00:18:22,621 --> 00:18:24,036
Maxim machine gun.

221
00:18:24,037 --> 00:18:29,620
Allowed to keep the rights to his invention, Maxim proceeded to license

222
00:18:29,621 --> 00:18:33,441
his weapon to Russia and Germany.

223
00:18:33,442 --> 00:18:40,620
Germany immediately saw the potential for a rapid-fire weapon,

224
00:18:40,621 --> 00:18:45,620
and by 1908, they were mass-producing the Maxim machine gun under the name Maschinengewehr,

225
00:18:45,621 --> 00:18:47,460
or MG08.

226
00:18:49,360 --> 00:18:50,380
Unbeknownst to Maxim,

227
00:18:50,780 --> 00:18:52,620
he had sold his rapid-fire killing machine

228
00:18:52,621 --> 00:18:54,300
to countries that would soon be enemies

229
00:18:54,301 --> 00:18:55,420
in the First World War.

230
00:18:56,920 --> 00:18:59,880
This is the Maxim machine gun.

231
00:19:00,440 --> 00:19:02,540
This is the German model 1908.

232
00:19:03,540 --> 00:19:07,000
And this was the standard machine gun

233
00:19:07,001 --> 00:19:08,520
of the German Army in World War I.

234
00:19:08,940 --> 00:19:10,060
And this large thing,

235
00:19:10,300 --> 00:19:12,660
that people at first thought it was an actual barrel,

236
00:19:12,780 --> 00:19:14,140
but this is actually a water jacket

237
00:19:14,141 --> 00:19:15,420
that goes around the barrel.

238
00:19:15,620 --> 00:19:20,840
The barrel gets so hot during the firing, the water cools it and keeps it from warping.

239
00:19:21,860 --> 00:19:25,380
The ammunition would come in belts of 250 cartridges.

240
00:19:26,340 --> 00:19:32,340
It would be steered into the feed here and then cock it like this,

241
00:19:33,140 --> 00:19:37,460
pull the belt in another time, cock it again.

242
00:19:38,060 --> 00:19:39,220
Now you're ready to go.

243
00:19:40,040 --> 00:19:42,960
Just pull the thumbs on the trigger and you're firing.

244
00:19:42,961 --> 00:19:47,300
and if all the conditions are right, you can fire 500 rounds in a minute.

245
00:19:51,780 --> 00:19:56,980
World War I created an arena in which the Vickers and MG08 were pitted against one another.

246
00:19:59,820 --> 00:20:01,880
And although they were identical in design,

247
00:20:02,580 --> 00:20:05,980
the numbers available at the onset of the war were completely lopsided.

248
00:20:05,981 --> 00:20:12,680
the most organized and disciplined and clear thinking force

249
00:20:12,681 --> 00:20:15,460
in the European theater were the Germans.

250
00:20:16,180 --> 00:20:18,760
And they had seen the importance of the machine gun.

251
00:20:18,761 --> 00:20:22,800
They already had 12,500 Maxims,

252
00:20:23,060 --> 00:20:26,140
and it took the British forever to get them organized

253
00:20:26,141 --> 00:20:30,740
and flowing at the rate that the Germans had organized them and produced them.

254
00:20:30,741 --> 00:20:38,640
In September 1914, the German offensive into France was stopped at the Battle of the Marne.

255
00:20:39,940 --> 00:20:45,800
The Germans, now in a defensive mode, demonstrated the tactical importance of the Maxim machine gun.

256
00:20:47,180 --> 00:20:55,060
The homelands to their rear and dug in, the machine gun proves to be the ideal weapon against enemy attackers.

257
00:20:55,061 --> 00:21:05,559
The role of the machine gun was to lay down tremendous direct firepower that until you could learn how to outflank it and to beat it by maneuver, drove everybody into

258
00:21:05,560 --> 00:21:07,520
trenches and stagnated the war.

259
00:21:07,940 --> 00:21:15,620
A machine gun could wipe out an entire group of men very quickly, thousands and thousands of men in a very short time.

260
00:21:15,621 --> 00:21:23,900
Although field commanders knew the dangers of facing machine gun fire in the heavily barbed-wired no-man's land,

261
00:21:24,560 --> 00:21:27,000
it did little to change their steadfast tactics.

262
00:21:27,480 --> 00:21:35,160
The mentality of stand up and face your enemy and fight was still in the minds of commanders during World War I.

263
00:21:35,400 --> 00:21:42,280
It was madness to think that the infantry were still the prime way of carrying a position

264
00:21:42,281 --> 00:21:46,060
simply by walking into the face of machine guns,

265
00:21:46,340 --> 00:21:48,680
which is exactly the tactics that the British espoused.

266
00:21:50,160 --> 00:21:54,140
In no other battle of the war did the Maxim machine gun spill more blood

267
00:21:54,141 --> 00:21:57,800
than at the Battle of the Somme in July of 1916.

268
00:21:58,900 --> 00:22:01,340
The British commander, Sir Douglas Haig,

269
00:22:01,341 --> 00:22:07,800
calculated that by bombarding the German positions with enough artillery fire,

270
00:22:08,300 --> 00:22:11,060
he could cut the German defensive wire,

271
00:22:11,061 --> 00:22:13,700
force the Germans underground

272
00:22:13,701 --> 00:22:16,480
and that the British infantry then

273
00:22:16,481 --> 00:22:19,780
could charge through the cuts in the wire

274
00:22:19,781 --> 00:22:21,740
and get through the German lines

275
00:22:21,741 --> 00:22:24,340
before the day's survivors could pop up

276
00:22:24,341 --> 00:22:25,980
from their shelters underground.

277
00:22:26,860 --> 00:22:28,160
Sir Douglas Hay goes wrong.

278
00:22:29,380 --> 00:22:33,820
The bombardment did not cut enough of the wire.

279
00:22:34,560 --> 00:22:37,420
The Germans had prepared in advance

280
00:22:37,421 --> 00:22:41,200
positions 50 feet or more underground

281
00:22:41,201 --> 00:22:44,400
so that they could survive this terrific bombardment.

282
00:22:50,760 --> 00:22:52,980
After eight days of constant shelling,

283
00:22:53,340 --> 00:22:56,380
British Commander Douglas Haig sent his troops out of their trenches

284
00:22:56,381 --> 00:22:57,800
to attack the German front.

285
00:22:59,760 --> 00:23:02,320
The queue to the Germans was the break.

286
00:23:02,460 --> 00:23:04,380
When the artillery barrage stopped,

287
00:23:05,040 --> 00:23:07,960
They'd been in this war for almost two years already.

288
00:23:08,320 --> 00:23:09,440
They knew what was coming.

289
00:23:09,760 --> 00:23:12,200
It was not a hard thing for the Germans to figure out.

290
00:23:13,980 --> 00:23:18,120
Armed and ready, German machine gunners mowed down the attacking British infantry,

291
00:23:18,960 --> 00:23:20,920
killing thousands in just the first minutes.

292
00:23:24,020 --> 00:23:28,780
At day's end, 57,000 British soldiers were either wounded or killed.

293
00:23:28,781 --> 00:23:37,340
A dark one-day record in British warfare that still stands today.

294
00:23:41,220 --> 00:23:44,540
For the next four months, the Battle of the Somme raged.

295
00:23:50,640 --> 00:23:53,180
Eventually, the Allies declared victory.

296
00:23:53,181 --> 00:24:00,900
But the carnage left behind amounted to nearly one million British, French and German soldiers dead.

297
00:24:03,480 --> 00:24:09,280
Many falling to their deaths under a hail of bullets from one of the deadliest weapons ever created.

298
00:24:10,300 --> 00:24:11,520
The Maxim machine gun.

299
00:24:16,220 --> 00:24:19,260
Because Hiram Maxim patented his machine gun in Britain,

300
00:24:19,261 --> 00:24:24,980
Germany was forced to pay a fee to the British government for every Maxim machine gun it produced.

301
00:24:26,780 --> 00:24:31,920
Deadliest Weapons will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

302
00:24:40,800 --> 00:24:46,900
Since the dawn of organized warfare, fire has been employed on the battlefield with deadly results.

303
00:24:49,260 --> 00:24:56,220
But in World War II, fire in the form of incendiary bombs reached a new level of lethality

304
00:24:56,221 --> 00:25:00,320
by literally transforming cities into hell on Earth.

305
00:25:05,680 --> 00:25:08,540
General Sherman gave us the aphorism, war is hell.

306
00:25:08,840 --> 00:25:14,820
But it wasn't until decades after his death that war reached levels of hell that he could not begin to fathom.

307
00:25:14,821 --> 00:25:21,860
In the Second World War, Air Force commanders had a wide range of specialized bombs at their disposal.

308
00:25:27,940 --> 00:25:34,320
However, if the strategic goal was to destroy an entire city, the preferred bomb was the incendiary.

309
00:25:36,560 --> 00:25:42,280
An incendiary bomb typically is composed of a lot of very small devices weighing only a few pounds.

310
00:25:42,281 --> 00:25:46,720
They're designed to start a fire, and more importantly, to start a fire that turns into a much bigger fire.

311
00:25:47,920 --> 00:25:50,740
Weapon. Incendiary bomb.

312
00:25:51,300 --> 00:25:56,940
Primary incendiary fuels, thermite, magnesium, napalm, and phosphorus.

313
00:25:59,320 --> 00:26:03,840
The World War II incendiary bomb resembled a long stick filled with fuel,

314
00:26:04,520 --> 00:26:07,760
which was weighted down at one end to provide somewhat accurate delivery.

315
00:26:08,620 --> 00:26:13,440
The bomb was either dropped as a single projectile or packed inside a casing,

316
00:26:13,840 --> 00:26:18,680
which would break apart before impact, dispersing hundreds of incendiary sticks in a cluster.

317
00:26:20,860 --> 00:26:24,700
Upon impact, a heat-producing chemical activated the incendiary fuel.

318
00:26:29,880 --> 00:26:34,520
One large bomber could carry over 3,000 four-pound incendiary sticks,

319
00:26:34,880 --> 00:26:37,180
capable of starting thousands of fires.

320
00:26:37,760 --> 00:26:42,140
Under ideal conditions, these fires would join together and create a firestorm.

321
00:26:44,540 --> 00:26:49,740
In the early months of 1945, Nazi Germany was on the verge of collapse.

322
00:26:51,080 --> 00:26:55,300
Russian troops coming from the east, coupled with their allies coming from the west,

323
00:26:55,820 --> 00:26:57,680
planned to squeeze Germany into submission.

324
00:26:59,340 --> 00:27:03,360
However, the Germans have just launched the Ardennes offensive,

325
00:27:03,361 --> 00:27:06,540
which shocked the socks off of all the Allied leaders.

326
00:27:06,541 --> 00:27:13,160
It was that shock which prompted the change in the bombing campaigns, particularly the one that led to Dresden.

327
00:27:15,440 --> 00:27:19,580
Britain had two strategic goals in attacking the city of Dresden, Germany.

328
00:27:21,940 --> 00:27:26,000
One was to aid the Russian invasion by disarming the city's communications.

329
00:27:29,840 --> 00:27:34,920
The second was to destroy German morale by killing large numbers of civilians.

330
00:27:36,540 --> 00:27:44,440
The British shifted fundamentally to a policy of what they called area or morale bombing.

331
00:27:45,040 --> 00:27:51,400
As one British writer put it, the campaign of morale bombing was really a cosmetic term for massacre.

332
00:27:53,540 --> 00:27:58,940
Winston Churchill's command to use incendiaries against Germany was carried out with great success

333
00:27:58,941 --> 00:28:02,440
by Royal Air Force Commander Arthur Bomber Harris.

334
00:28:03,160 --> 00:28:04,740
Harris was the true believer.

335
00:28:04,741 --> 00:28:12,320
Harris felt that attacking German cities would not just inconvenience the Germans, it would bring them down.

336
00:28:14,360 --> 00:28:21,980
On the night of February 13, 1945, 250 British Lancasters flying at low altitude

337
00:28:21,981 --> 00:28:25,500
attacked Dresden with the intent of creating a firestorm.

338
00:28:26,720 --> 00:28:29,880
The British really perfected the recipe for incendiary attack.

339
00:28:29,881 --> 00:28:34,480
They would first send in a small group of planes as pathfinders to mark the aiming point.

340
00:28:34,740 --> 00:28:39,580
Behind those planes came the main force, which were aircraft loaded with high explosive bombs,

341
00:28:39,660 --> 00:28:43,540
which were designed to blow the roofs off of buildings and otherwise shatter the structure

342
00:28:43,541 --> 00:28:47,480
to make them kindling for the large bundles of incendiaries that followed.

343
00:28:47,481 --> 00:28:51,060
And this worked to perfection in creating large fires.

344
00:28:52,220 --> 00:28:55,180
During the first raid, which lasted 15 minutes,

345
00:28:55,720 --> 00:28:59,340
over 300 tons of thermite incendiaries were dropped on Dresden.

346
00:28:59,341 --> 00:29:03,360
Minutes after the bombardment stopped,

347
00:29:04,240 --> 00:29:07,500
thousands of fires came together to form a massive firestorm.

348
00:29:08,780 --> 00:29:13,960
That fire thrusts up a fountain column of incandescent gases

349
00:29:13,961 --> 00:29:16,180
and burning debris which spread the fire.

350
00:29:16,440 --> 00:29:18,140
But more importantly, it acts like a bell.

351
00:29:18,220 --> 00:29:22,780
It sucks in air and oxygen into the fire at winds of gale force,

352
00:29:22,781 --> 00:29:25,680
which frequently will suck individuals back into the fire.

353
00:29:25,681 --> 00:29:34,160
temperatures inside the core of the firestorm reached upwards of 1500 degrees celsius you had

354
00:29:34,161 --> 00:29:43,160
the most horrific scenes of you know thousands of women babies trapped in these infernos with these

355
00:29:43,161 --> 00:29:46,980
superheated hundred mile an hour winds burning them up

356
00:29:46,981 --> 00:29:54,920
Ten square miles of Dresden were completely destroyed.

357
00:29:56,060 --> 00:30:02,940
The number of people killed is unknown, but estimates range from 25,000 to 40,000.

358
00:30:06,840 --> 00:30:11,600
As horrific as the burning of Dresden was, it was only a taste of what was to come.

359
00:30:11,601 --> 00:30:18,220
Less than a month later, the United States began firebombing Japanese cities,

360
00:30:18,780 --> 00:30:21,940
utilizing a new incendiary fuel called napalm.

361
00:30:22,440 --> 00:30:28,900
The Army Air Force realized early in the war that their two incendiary bombs relied upon either rubber or magnesium,

362
00:30:29,260 --> 00:30:30,900
both of which were in terribly short supply.

363
00:30:31,840 --> 00:30:36,340
They came up with a compound of naphthenic and palmitic acids,

364
00:30:36,720 --> 00:30:41,100
which were contracted to be called napalm, which was mixed with gasoline,

365
00:30:41,101 --> 00:30:44,740
and produced by far the most effective incendiary device of World War II.

366
00:30:46,560 --> 00:30:48,680
Napalm burns with extreme intensity,

367
00:30:49,180 --> 00:30:51,420
and its stringy and sticky consistency

368
00:30:51,421 --> 00:30:54,740
allows it to spread more effectively than other incendiaries.

369
00:30:58,180 --> 00:30:59,960
By March of 1945,

370
00:31:00,940 --> 00:31:03,600
Curtis LeMay, in charge of the 21st Bomber Command,

371
00:31:04,280 --> 00:31:06,420
concluded that high-altitude daytime bombing

372
00:31:06,421 --> 00:31:10,060
was simply not accurate enough to be effective against Japanese targets.

373
00:31:11,100 --> 00:31:18,600
Curtis LeMay directed his crewmen to fly not in the daytime, but at night.

374
00:31:20,040 --> 00:31:25,880
Much of Japanese industry was cottage industry scattered out in the cities,

375
00:31:26,560 --> 00:31:28,940
and so LeMay aimed at the cities themselves.

376
00:31:29,780 --> 00:31:34,420
Burn the cities down, and you would destroy Japanese war industry,

377
00:31:34,820 --> 00:31:39,440
and at the same time, you might so horrify the Japanese leadership

378
00:31:39,441 --> 00:31:41,640
that it would give up.

379
00:31:43,320 --> 00:31:49,220
On March 9th, 330 B-29 bombers took off from the Mariana Islands,

380
00:31:49,540 --> 00:31:50,740
headed for Tokyo, Japan.

381
00:31:53,260 --> 00:31:55,440
After two hours of the bombardment,

382
00:31:56,020 --> 00:31:59,020
the wooden city of Tokyo was engulfed in a firestorm.

383
00:31:59,640 --> 00:32:04,400
These fires were so hot that they would literally ignite the clothing of individuals as they were fleeing.

384
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:09,140
What was particularly horrifying was a lot of the women wore what they called air raid turbans

385
00:32:09,141 --> 00:32:12,680
around their heads, and the heat would ignite those turbines

386
00:32:12,681 --> 00:32:16,920
like igniting the wick on a candle to start consuming them with flame.

387
00:32:18,500 --> 00:32:22,700
The aftermath of the incendiary bombings on the evenings of March 9th and 10th

388
00:32:22,701 --> 00:32:25,960
led to an estimated 100,000 Japanese killed.

389
00:32:27,920 --> 00:32:33,020
The night raid against Tokyo was the most devastating single raid

390
00:32:33,021 --> 00:32:35,980
ever carried out by aircraft in any war,

391
00:32:35,981 --> 00:32:40,580
to include the nuclear bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki.

392
00:32:41,620 --> 00:32:44,400
Once the success of that campaign became clear,

393
00:32:44,900 --> 00:32:50,500
the Army Air Force then simply got out a list of the 180 largest Japanese cities

394
00:32:50,501 --> 00:32:56,060
and began firebombing every single city on that list to produce an end to the war.

395
00:32:56,860 --> 00:32:59,180
At the end of the firebombing campaign,

396
00:32:59,860 --> 00:33:04,540
64 Japanese cities and nearly 200 square miles were burned to the ground.

397
00:33:04,541 --> 00:33:09,240
The final death toll was estimated at 200,000.

398
00:33:14,780 --> 00:33:17,660
Technological advancements in precision-guided missiles

399
00:33:17,661 --> 00:33:20,500
eventually put an end to incendiary carpet bombing.

400
00:33:23,020 --> 00:33:27,560
But the legacy of the Allied firebombing campaigns against Germany and Japan

401
00:33:27,561 --> 00:33:30,700
and the tremendous loss of life associated with them

402
00:33:30,701 --> 00:33:33,480
firmly established the incendiary bomb

403
00:33:33,481 --> 00:33:36,860
is one of the deadliest weapons ever used in battle.

404
00:33:42,080 --> 00:33:46,720
In 1980, a United Nations convention banned the use of napalm

405
00:33:46,721 --> 00:33:49,300
and other incendiaries against civilian populations.

406
00:33:50,140 --> 00:33:52,740
The United States refused to ratify the treaty

407
00:33:52,741 --> 00:33:55,360
and continues to use incendiaries.

408
00:33:57,060 --> 00:34:00,100
Deadliest Weapons will return on Modern Marvels.

409
00:34:03,480 --> 00:34:16,040
In World War II, the United States and Japan engaged in a series of carrier battles that would play a crucial role in deciding the battle for the Pacific.

410
00:34:17,540 --> 00:34:19,832
With the outcome in doubt, the U.S.

411
00:34:19,833 --> 00:34:27,100
Navy unleashed a secret weapon called the VT Fuse, which devastated Japanese aircraft with unmatched lethality.

412
00:34:27,101 --> 00:34:33,040
If you were making a list of the most important technological breakthroughs that came out of World War II,

413
00:34:33,540 --> 00:34:37,520
just below the atomic bomb and radar in general, you would list the VT fuse,

414
00:34:37,780 --> 00:34:41,060
which absolutely transformed the effectiveness of anti-aircraft fire.

415
00:34:42,380 --> 00:34:45,480
Before the U.S. became involved in the Second World War,

416
00:34:45,960 --> 00:34:50,540
the Navy realized that a revolutionary advance in anti-aircraft fuses would be necessary

417
00:34:50,541 --> 00:34:53,100
to combat the growing effectiveness of the airplane.

418
00:34:53,101 --> 00:35:03,060
To shoot down an airplane required an average of 2,400 rounds of ammunition early in the war.

419
00:35:05,140 --> 00:35:12,840
Hitting an aircraft, moving at high speed in three dimensions with a gun on a ship, is one of the most difficult feats in warfare.

420
00:35:13,440 --> 00:35:15,920
There's simply too many variables to be accurately predicted.

421
00:35:15,921 --> 00:35:23,980
The difficulty in shooting down enemy aircraft was largely due to the inadequacies associated with anti-aircraft fuses.

422
00:35:26,780 --> 00:35:35,280
A fuse is either a mechanical or electronic detonating device designed to set off the bursting charge of a projectile, bomb or torpedo.

423
00:35:38,780 --> 00:35:43,060
Although all fuses serve the same function, the triggering mechanism varies.

424
00:35:43,980 --> 00:35:48,320
Two different types of fuses were available to the major navies.

425
00:35:48,680 --> 00:35:51,320
One type of fuse was the contact fuse.

426
00:35:51,800 --> 00:35:55,560
That is, the projectile actually had to hit the aircraft

427
00:35:55,561 --> 00:35:58,360
for the fuse to detonate the projectile.

428
00:35:59,040 --> 00:36:02,000
The chances of hitting an aircraft were slender.

429
00:36:02,001 --> 00:36:06,720
A better fuse was a fuse activated by time.

430
00:36:07,220 --> 00:36:12,040
The gunners would set the fuse as the projectile was loaded into the gun.

431
00:36:12,041 --> 00:36:18,260
The fuse would detonate after a predetermined time.

432
00:36:18,920 --> 00:36:22,580
If your guesstimate is off by even a couple of seconds,

433
00:36:22,720 --> 00:36:26,020
the projectile will be hopelessly out of range of the aircraft.

434
00:36:27,000 --> 00:36:31,020
The remedy was to create a fuse that used a high-frequency signal

435
00:36:31,021 --> 00:36:33,480
to detonate within proximity of the target.

436
00:36:37,440 --> 00:36:39,980
Weapon. Proximity fuse.

437
00:36:40,960 --> 00:36:44,380
Detonation method. Remote sensing of target.

438
00:36:46,140 --> 00:36:51,040
The task of finding a high-frequency mechanism small enough to fit on an artillery shell

439
00:36:51,041 --> 00:36:56,420
and durable enough to withstand the 20,000 G-force associated with being shot out of a gun

440
00:36:56,421 --> 00:36:59,200
were believed to be too difficult to overcome.

441
00:37:02,180 --> 00:37:05,720
But at the Applied Physics Laboratory at Johns Hopkins University,

442
00:37:05,721 --> 00:37:13,540
Physicist Merle Toove and a group of scientists, engineers and ham radio experts embraced the challenge.

443
00:37:15,540 --> 00:37:23,000
Merle was a genius. He was a rough, tough, slightly profane individual.

444
00:37:24,580 --> 00:37:29,900
I hated the man, but hate and love are very close.

445
00:37:29,901 --> 00:37:38,580
Early in the program, TUV determined that using radio frequencies would be the most practical way to create an effective proximity fuse.

446
00:37:42,000 --> 00:37:50,980
A radio proximity fuse works by emitting radio frequencies from an oscillator, which bounce off the reflective surface of an object, such as an enemy aircraft.

447
00:37:52,860 --> 00:37:59,480
The reflected pulse then returns amplified, triggering the fuse detonator and exploding the projectile.

448
00:37:59,900 --> 00:38:05,960
If detonation occurs close enough to the target, fragments from the exploding shell will destroy it.

449
00:38:06,560 --> 00:38:16,120
The problems that were faced in the development of a proximity fuse were first to choose the mechanism, the type of fuse.

450
00:38:16,660 --> 00:38:23,760
We do not have transistors and therefore we're dependent upon vacuum tubes.

451
00:38:23,761 --> 00:38:29,080
Vacuum tubes were not intended for this class of environment.

452
00:38:29,780 --> 00:38:43,780
However, early experimentation found that there was at least the potential for making the commercial sub-miniature vacuum tubes withstand that environment.

453
00:38:45,040 --> 00:38:53,980
In 1941, miniature commercial vacuum tubes engineered by Sylvania and others were sent to the Applied Physics Laboratory.

454
00:38:55,220 --> 00:39:01,140
There, they were incorporated into the first test fuses, named the Mark 32 VT fuse.

455
00:39:01,980 --> 00:39:12,180
Every day we would build perhaps 30 fuse units that would be driven to the test field and fired the following day.

456
00:39:13,000 --> 00:39:19,820
Meanwhile, we would be building another group of fuses according to a new prescription.

457
00:39:21,220 --> 00:39:28,900
Under a veil of secrecy, the Applied Physics Laboratory worked on perfecting the radio proximity fuse for the next 18 months.

458
00:39:30,500 --> 00:39:38,980
In January 1942, the VT fuse reached a success rate of 50% when within proximity of its target.

459
00:39:38,981 --> 00:39:45,580
Deemed more effective than the saturation shelling needed for contact and time fuses,

460
00:39:46,520 --> 00:39:50,800
the U.S. Ordnance Committee fully endorsed the VT fuse project.

461
00:39:53,140 --> 00:39:55,340
Mass production began immediately.

462
00:39:55,880 --> 00:39:57,640
We didn't work eight-hour days.

463
00:39:57,641 --> 00:39:59,980
We worked whatever the job called for.

464
00:40:00,680 --> 00:40:03,420
On one occasion, I worked for 48 hours straight.

465
00:40:03,421 --> 00:40:11,640
and everybody else desperately wanted to contribute toward the winning of that war.

466
00:40:12,380 --> 00:40:13,840
The pressures were huge.

467
00:40:14,680 --> 00:40:22,500
In January of 1943, a naval anti-aircraft gun equipped with the first batch of proximity-fused projectiles

468
00:40:22,501 --> 00:40:25,120
shot down an attacking Japanese plane.

469
00:40:26,700 --> 00:40:29,320
One target, one kill.

470
00:40:30,420 --> 00:40:32,180
The proximity fuse had arrived.

471
00:40:33,420 --> 00:40:37,600
Earlier, it took 2,400 shells to bring down an aircraft.

472
00:40:38,140 --> 00:40:40,440
The number now was 400.

473
00:40:41,020 --> 00:40:47,480
So you have a six-fold increase in anti-aircraft effectiveness with this fuse.

474
00:40:49,280 --> 00:40:52,640
Proximity fuses were quickly sent to the naval fleet in the Pacific.

475
00:40:54,760 --> 00:40:59,800
In the battle for the Philippine Sea, the fuse decimated Japanese aircraft.

476
00:40:59,801 --> 00:41:05,100
Every one of the American ships was loaded with radio proximity fuses.

477
00:41:05,780 --> 00:41:09,180
They shot down Japanese planes by the hundreds.

478
00:41:09,760 --> 00:41:17,020
395 planes were lost by the Japanese, which was 92% of the planes they brought to the event.

479
00:41:18,120 --> 00:41:22,940
The battle was the culminating battle of the Pacific.

480
00:41:22,941 --> 00:41:36,840
But concerns about America's secret weapon falling into enemy hands led the Navy to disallow its use over land until December of 1944.

481
00:41:38,520 --> 00:41:44,180
At the Battle of the Bulge, VT-fused artillery shells laid waste to the Germans.

482
00:41:44,181 --> 00:41:54,500
The first time it was used, it stopped a German push about 50 miles into the bulge, and about 400 Germans were killed.

483
00:41:54,501 --> 00:42:03,540
projectiles would come in and they would burst at a relatively uniform height in synchronization

484
00:42:03,541 --> 00:42:11,020
and you would have this immense barrage of exploding shells just above the ground

485
00:42:11,021 --> 00:42:16,340
spraying the hail of shrapnel that was deadly.

486
00:42:16,341 --> 00:42:23,440
The lethal impact of America's secret weapon was perhaps summed up best by a letter from

487
00:42:23,441 --> 00:42:25,420
General George S. Patton, who wrote,

488
00:42:26,400 --> 00:42:29,260
The new shell with the funny fuse is devastating.

489
00:42:30,080 --> 00:42:34,280
I think that when all armies get this shell, we will have to devise some new method of

490
00:42:34,281 --> 00:42:34,981
warfare.

491
00:42:36,600 --> 00:42:42,920
Patton's formal testimony officially solidified the VT fuse as one of World War II's deadliest

492
00:42:42,921 --> 00:42:43,621
weapons.

493
00:42:46,340 --> 00:42:55,360
The violent century that gave rise to the deadliest weapons the world has ever seen

494
00:42:55,361 --> 00:43:00,160
has also left behind a surplus of lesser weapons available on the black market

495
00:43:00,161 --> 00:43:02,980
that may prove to be just as deadly.

496
00:43:04,340 --> 00:43:08,120
Small arms and light weapons take the lives of hundreds of thousands of people each year

497
00:43:08,121 --> 00:43:11,800
in ordinary crime and also in conflict zones.

498
00:43:11,801 --> 00:43:13,800
They perpetuate conflict.

499
00:43:14,460 --> 00:43:17,940
There's 630 million of these weapons in circulation today.

500
00:43:18,080 --> 00:43:21,060
Now that's in government stockpiles as well as on the black market.

501
00:43:22,440 --> 00:43:30,000
In 1993, Somalian insurgents demonstrated what a well-funded and well-armed militia is capable of achieving.

502
00:43:31,640 --> 00:43:34,840
Somali militiamen using only small arms and light weapons

503
00:43:34,841 --> 00:43:40,480
forced the United States and its billion dollars worth of sophisticated military technology from Somalia.

504
00:43:40,481 --> 00:43:43,800
The decisive weapon? The rocket-propelled grenade.

505
00:43:44,260 --> 00:43:49,380
40-year-old Soviet military technology that can be acquired on the black market often for less than $1,000.

506
00:43:50,180 --> 00:44:00,220
The proliferation of the small arms black market has paved the way for well-funded terrorist groups and rogue nations to kill indiscriminately and without consequence.

507
00:44:01,380 --> 00:44:05,720
But what will the consequences be if they get access to the world's deadliest weapon?

508
00:44:05,721 --> 00:44:11,480
If uranium enrichment becomes very easy, lots of countries can build nuclear weapons.

509
00:44:12,160 --> 00:44:16,060
And what will the world look like when anybody can kill everybody?

510
00:44:17,680 --> 00:44:23,680
The result may be an even deadlier world, where nuclear weapons may no longer deter,

511
00:44:24,460 --> 00:44:29,320
and all it takes to kill hundreds of thousands is a cause and currency.

